      "title": "Ollama v0.40.5",
      "link": "https://github.com/ollama/ollama/releases/tag/v0.40.5",
      "summary": "NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Super NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Super is a vision-language model for coding, reasoning, tool use, and document understanding. It pairs a 120B-parameter hybrid Mamba-2/Transformer mixture-of-experts backbone (12B active per token) with a C-RADIO vision encoder, and supports tool calling, an on/off thinking mode, and context up to 1M tokens. ollama run nemotron-3.5-super A compact sibling, Nemotron 3.5 Bolt , distills Super down to 74B parameters (9B active) tuned to fit a 24 GB NVIDIA GPU for everyday agentic tasks, with speculative decoding via its included drafter. What's Changed Decision models are now available on cloud — use clef:cloud and clef-flash:cloud to run decision models through ollama.com cloud without pulling them locally Continuing an OpenAI-compatible Responses conversation that includes custom tool calls no longer fails with an error Clearer error messages when cloud access fails: sign-in problems, payment issues in the desktop app, and upstream access errors from the ChatGPT gateway now explain what went wrong Updated llama.cpp (adding Nemotron 3.5 support) and MLX New Contributors @willietran made their first contribution in #18986 Full Changelog : ",
      "source": "GitHub (Ollama)",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-15T19:18:39+00:00",
      "releaseTag": "v0.40.5",
      "isPrerelease": false,
      "section_hint": "AI",
      "articleText": "## NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Super\r\n\r\n[NVIDIA Nemotron 3.5 Super](https://ollama.com/library/nemotron-3.5-super) is a vision-language model for coding, reasoning, tool use, and document understanding. It pairs a 120B-parameter hybrid Mamba-2/Transformer mixture-of-experts backbone (12B active per token) with a C-RADIO vision encoder, and supports tool calling, an on/off thinking mode, and context up to 1M tokens.\r\n\r\n```\r\nollama run nemotron-3.5-super\r\n```\r\n\r\nA compact sibling, [Nemotron 3.5 Bolt](https://ollama.com/library/nemotron-3.5-bolt), distills Super down to 74B parameters (9B active) tuned to fit a 24 GB NVIDIA GPU for everyday agentic tasks, with speculative decoding via its included drafter.\r\n\r\n## What's Changed\r\n* Decision models are now available on cloud — use `clef:cloud` and `clef-flash:cloud` to run decision models through ollama.com cloud without pulling them locally\r\n* Continuing an OpenAI-compatible Responses conversation that includes custom tool calls no longer fails with an error\r\n* Clearer error messages when cloud access fails: sign-in problems, payment issues in the desktop app, and upstream access errors from the ChatGPT gateway now explain what went wrong\r\n* Updated llama.cpp (adding Nemotron 3.5 support) and MLX\r\n\r\n## New Contributors\r\n* @willietran made their first contribution in https://github.com/ollama/ollama/pull/18986\r\n\r\n**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/ollama/ollama/compare/v0.40.4...v0.40.5",
      "articleUrl": "https://api.github.com/repos/ollama/ollama/releases/tags/v0.40.5",
      "articleFetchedAt": "2026-10-15T23:22:13.692075+00:00",
      "articleStatus": "fetched",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-14T19:45:22Z"
    },
    {
      "title": "Stryker invests $400M into CMR Surgical",
      "link": "https://www.therobotreport.com/stryker-invests-400m-into-cmr-surgical/",
      "summary": "Stryker plans to serve as the exclusive sales agent in the U.S. for the Versius robotics portfolio from CMR Surgical. The post Stryker invests $400M into CMR Surgical appeared first on The Robot Report .",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-15T16:54:14+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-15T16:54:14+00:00",
      "source": "The Robot Report",
      "section_hint": "트렌드",
      "articleText": "CMR Surgical said it designed Versius to be compact and modular, so it doesn’t require a dedicated OR. | Source: CMR Surgical Stryker this week said it has invested about $400 million into CMR Surgical, with an exclusive option for it to acquire the company’s remaining equity. Stryker also secured an exclusive U.S. commercial agency agreement for the Versius portfolio. “This strategic alliance is a significant milestone for CMR and a strong endorsement of the technology, clinical experience, and global business we have built,” stated Massimiliano Colella, CEO of CMR Surgical. “Stryker’s investment will give us additional strength to accelerate innovation and the continued global expansion of the Versius portfolio, while its scale and deep customer relationships create a significant growth opportunity in the United States.” Cambridge, U.K.-based CMR Surgical said it has designed the Versius platform for real-world operating environments. The surgical robot ‘s modular architecture supports flexibility across procedures, surgeon access and sites of care, the company claimed. CMR Surgical gets clearance for more procedures CMR Surgical said that Versius can help healthcare providers grow robotic adoption within existing operating room workflows. This scalable approach can help health systems meet growing demand for robotic-assisted surgery while preserving operational flexibility and supporting long-term program economics, added the company. Versius supports procedures including general surgery, colorectal surgery, urology, gynecology and thoracic surgery. The robot has has been adopted in hospitals across more than 30 countries and used in 45,000 cases worldwide, according to CMR. The Versius Plus system has earned FDA 510(k) clearance for cholecystectomy. CMR has also filed a 510(k) submission for benign gynecological indications. The robot has been used in 45,000 cases worldwide, it said. Stryker to sell Versius system in the U.S. “Soft-tissue robotics is a high-growth adjacency and a natural extension of Stryker’s strengths in endoscopy, visualization, and enabling technologies,” stated Andy Pierce, the group president of medsurg and neurotechnology at Stryker. “We have evaluated the segment carefully for years, and this alliance represents a thoughtful and disciplined entry that combines the complementary strengths of both companies,” he added. “We selected CMR Surgical and the Versius portfolio because of the opportunity to help healthcare providers grow robotic-assisted surgery adoption across a broad range of procedures in hospitals and ASCs.” “We also look forward to welcoming Andy Pierce to our board of directors and benefiting from his more than 30 years of MedTech leadership experience at Stryker as we continue expanding access to robotic-assisted surgery globally,” said Colella. Moving forward, Stryker will serve as the exclusive U.S. sales agent for the Versius portfolio, while CMR Surgical will continue to lead portfolio development and commercialization in markets outside the United States. Stryker’s exclusive acquisition option gives it the option of buying 100% of CMR Surgical’s outstanding equity in the future, subject to the agreement’s terms. This transaction is subject to regulatory approvals and customary closing conditions. Register now and help us celebrate 20 years of RoboBusiness! Tell Us What You Think! Cancel reply This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed. Related Articles Read More > ForceN to give a crash course on force and torque sensing for humanoids at RoboBusiness Neptune Medical earns FDA clearance for its Triton 1 robotic system Report covers advances in surgical robots, deploying warehouse automation in the real world Enovis to acquire surgical tech provider eCential Robotics for $180M",
